<p style="font-size:14px; line-height:1.5; color:#555555; text-align:center; margin-top:-10px;">Gmail, Netflix, and Amazon are web apps your students already use daily, even if they've never thought about them that way.</p>

<hr style="border:none; border-top:1px solid #E4E2F8; margin:24px 0;">

<h2 >Key Components You'll See in Student Code</h2>

Every web app your students build this unit rests on the same three technologies, plus one new collaborator. 

- **HTML** gives a page its structure, the way framing gives a house its shape.
- **CSS** handles the style: colors, fonts, and layout, the finishes that make the house look like something.
- **JavaScript** adds interactivity and logic, the doors that open and the lights that turn on.
-  **AI** now sits alongside all three as a creative assistant, suggesting, refining, and debugging code while your students stay in the driver's seat on every decision.

<img src="https://images.code.org/68518643264a7b4d79b23a8bbf5b7720-image-1785853424364.png" alt="HTML is the structure of a webpage, like the frame of a house. CSS is the style and design, how the house looks. JavaScript is the interactivity and logic, like doors opening or lights turning on. AI is a creative assistant that helps suggest, refine, and debug code while students stay in control of decisions." width="100%" style="display:block; max-width:750px; margin:16px auto; border-radius:10px;">



